# {{ ansible_managed }}
# =============================================================================
# Netdata go.d — Docker collector (ADR-030, phase 2 groundwork)
# =============================================================================
# What it unlocks: the two conditions homelab-health.sh expresses as "unhealthy
# >10min" and "not running >10min". Those are the only host signals that have no
# equivalent anywhere else, since Kuma probes services from the outside and
# cannot see inside the engine at all.
#
# This file first shipped alone, deliberately, under the rule netdata.yml still
# states: a collector arrives without alarms, so a deploy only makes new charts
# appear and nothing can turn a monitor red on the strength of a collector
# nobody has watched. Its alarms arrive WITH it now, and the exception is worth
# justifying rather than hiding: the charts have been watched, on a throwaway
# agent against a throwaway proxy (below), and an alarm on a collector that
# fails cannot report healthy — no chart means no alarm instance, and the
# adapter reports a name it cannot find as "not loaded in netdata". The failure
# mode of shipping them together is loud, which is the property the rule exists
# to protect.
#
# The address is the socket-proxy, NOT /var/run/docker.sock as the stock config
# assumes: this container never gets the raw socket (ADR on the socket proxy),
# and DOCKER_HOST is already pointed there in compose.yaml.
#
# --- It did not work, and here is what it took (resolved 2026-08-23) --------
# Deployed first with the socket-proxy as it stood, this collector produced no
# context at all, and the running agent said why:
#
#   check failed: Error response from daemon: 403 Forbidden
#   Request forbidden by administrative rules.   collector=docker job=local
#
# The pre-flight test that made it look safe was too narrow — /version and
# /containers/json both answer 200, so the proxy looked open enough:
#
#   /version         200      /images/json   403
#   /info            200      /system/df     403
#   /containers/json 200      /_ping         200
#
# Reading that table, BOTH denied endpoints looked like prerequisites. Only one
# was. Measured on a throwaway proxy with IMAGES=1: /images/json answers 200,
# /system/df stays 403, and a throwaway agent against that proxy logged
#
#   check success   collector=docker job=probe
#
# `/system/df` is only called when `collect_container_size: yes`, which the job
# below sets to `no`. So the fix was one variable, not two, and the difference
# was one experiment away — the first diagnosis would have widened the boundary
# twice as far on the strength of a plausible reading.
#
# What the collector then produces, per container:
#
#   docker.container_state          running paused exited created restarting …
#   docker.container_health_status  healthy unhealthy not_running_unhealthy …
#
# which is exactly what the two curated alarms need. What it does NOT produce is
# anything about a container that does not exist: no container, no chart, no
# alarm instance. That half stays with the goss spec (ADR-032), which walks the
# service list from compose.yaml and therefore notices an absence.
#
# `collect_container_size: no` is kept from the stock config: computing per
# container disk usage walks the whole layer store, which on this host lives on
# the HDD and would be paid every collection interval.
#
# `update_every` is NOT a local tuning decision. It divides the sample floor in
# homelab_container_unhealthy, which is derived from it rather than written
# twice — see netdata_docker_update_every in group_vars for why the two cannot
# be separated, and what it cost that they were.
jobs:
  - name: local
    address: 'tcp://socket-proxy:2375'
    timeout: 5
    update_every: {{ netdata_docker_update_every }}
    collect_container_size: no
